import{_ as o,o as t,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.f04UcrbE.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"LINQ Semantics Contract","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"articles/linq-semantics.md","filePath":"articles/linq-semantics.md","lastUpdated":1794237764000}'),s={name:"articles/linq-semantics.md"};function r(d,e,i,c,l,u){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="linq-semantics-contract" tabindex="-1">LINQ Semantics Contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#linq-semantics-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LINQ Semantics Contract&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page is the normative contract of the LINQ compiler. It defines what every node of the query IR means, so that a query returns the same rows on any backend and an optimizer pass knows what it may rely on. The text below is included from the source file <code>kernel/Samidare/IR/SEMANTICS.md</code>, and the IR conformance test suite executes each rule.</p><p>For the same rules from the point of view of someone writing queries, with examples, see <a href="/Polars.NET/articles/linq#_8-query-semantics-matching-linq-to-objects">Query Semantics</a>.</p><p>The LINQ IR (<code>LogicalPlan</code>, <code>ScalarExpr</code>) is the contract between the LINQ frontend and every execution backend, today and later, such as a managed engine. The frontend translates LINQ into IR that means what the query means. A backend must evaluate each node exactly as this document says. The optimizer passes may rely only on these rules.</p><p>The rules are executable. The conformance test project builds plans by hand and runs each one on every engine in <code>Engines.all</code>, both as written and after <code>LogicalOptimizer.optimize</code>. A new backend joins the suite by implementing <code>IIrEngine</code>. When a rule changes, change this file, the doc comments in <code>LogicalIR.fs</code> and the conformance tests together.</p><h2 id="values-and-logic" tabindex="-1">Values and logic <a class="header-anchor" href="#values-and-logic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Values and logic&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Typed nulls.</strong> Every type has a null. <code>LiteralValue.Null</code> carries its type.</p><p><strong>Three-valued (Kleene) logic.</strong> <code>And</code>, <code>Or</code> and <code>Not</code> follow Kleene rules: <code>false AND null</code> is false, and <code>true OR null</code> is true. A comparison (<code>Eq</code>, <code>NotEq</code>, <code>Lt</code>, ...) with a null operand is null. So is arithmetic with a null operand.</p><p><strong>Two-valued equality.</strong> <code>EqMissing</code> and <code>NotEqMissing</code> treat null as equal to null and as different from every value. The frontend uses them for C# <code>==</code> / <code>!=</code> on nullable operands.</p><p><strong>Null tests in other nodes.</strong></p><ul><li><code>Conditional</code> takes its else branch when the test is null.</li><li><code>Coalesce</code> returns its first non-null argument.</li></ul><p><strong>C# null semantics are compiled, not built in.</strong> A lifted comparison that is false on null in C# becomes <code>Coalesce [cmp; false]</code>. A filter treats null as false, so <code>FilterNullSimplification</code> removes that wrapper where the predicate is positive: at the root, under <code>And</code> / <code>Or</code>, and in conditional branches. With <code>CoreConfig.UseRelationalNulls</code>, the frontend emits plain SQL comparisons instead.</p><p>A comparison lifted to null (a <code>bool?</code> result that is null on a null operand) is never emitted by the C# compiler; it comes only from VB or hand-built expression trees. The frontend does not translate it, so it is evaluated by .NET.</p><h2 id="floating-point-values" tabindex="-1">Floating-point values <a class="header-anchor" href="#floating-point-values" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Floating-point values&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Engine total order.</strong> IR comparisons over floating values (<code>Float16</code>, <code>Float32</code>, <code>Float64</code>) follow the engine&#39;s total order. NaN equals NaN and is greater than every number. <code>Unary IsNan</code> is true for NaN, false for a number and null for null.</p><p><strong>.NET semantics are compiled, not built in (<code>NaNSemantics</code>).</strong> For <code>double</code>, <code>float</code> and <code>Half</code> operands that may be NaN, the frontend guards comparisons:</p><ul><li><code>==</code> adds <code>AND NOT isnan(left)</code>;</li><li><code>!=</code> adds <code>OR isnan(left)</code>;</li><li><code>&lt;</code> and <code>&lt;=</code> add <code>AND right &lt;= +inf</code>;</li><li><code>&gt;</code> and <code>&gt;=</code> add <code>AND left &lt;= +inf</code>.</li></ul><p><code>x &lt;= +inf</code> is false only for NaN. It is one plain comparison, which predicate pushdown and statistics understand. So a comparison involving NaN is false, and <code>!=</code> is true.</p><p>A floating sort key sorts by <code>ScalarFunction.FloatOrderKey</code>, an Int64 whose order is .NET&#39;s order:</p><ul><li>NaN comes before every number;</li><li>-0.0 equals 0.0;</li><li>null stays null, so nulls keep the place the order gives them.</li></ul><p>This is a single key, as <code>Comparer&lt;T&gt;.Default</code> orders.</p><p><strong>Aggregates.</strong> <code>Min</code> is .NET <code>Min</code>: NaN as soon as one value is NaN. <code>Max</code> skips NaN unless every value is NaN. Grouping and <code>Distinct</code> treat NaN as one value (.NET <code>Equals</code>).</p><p><strong>Literals.</strong> <code>LiteralValue.Float16</code> holds a <code>Half</code>.</p><h2 id="arithmetic" tabindex="-1">Arithmetic <a class="header-anchor" href="#arithmetic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Arithmetic&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Truncating division and remainder.</strong> <code>Modulo</code> is the .NET remainder for every numeric type: it truncates, so the result carries the dividend&#39;s sign (-7 % 2 = -1, -7.5 % 2 = -1.5). <code>TruncatedDivide</code> is .NET integer division, which truncates toward zero (-7 / 2 = -3). <code>Divide</code> over integers is the engine&#39;s own division, so the frontend emits <code>TruncatedDivide</code> for integer <code>/</code>.</p><p>A backend may compute both in one pass.</p><p><strong>Decimals.</strong> Decimals are fixed-point. The frontend widens a decimal dividend to scale 18, so a quotient keeps 18 fractional digits (.NET keeps up to 28 significant digits). A decimal value has its column&#39;s scale, so its string form carries that scale (2.500, not 2.5), while its value is the same.</p><p><strong>Division by zero and overflow.</strong> Integer or decimal division and remainder by zero give null (.NET raises). The frontend guards a decimal divisor, because the engine raises. Integer overflow wraps, as unchecked .NET arithmetic does.</p><p><strong>Checked sums.</strong> .NET&#39;s <code>Sum</code> of <code>int</code> / <code>long</code>, and the <code>long</code> sum behind <code>Average</code> of <code>long</code>, are checked. Over all the rows, the frontend emits <code>AggKind.CheckedSum</code>: the exact total in the summed type, failing with <code>QueryErrorKind.Overflow</code> (a backend may compute it in one pass). Per group (a GroupBy&#39;s aggregations, a window), it composes plain aggregations, which the engine computes for every group at once, and asserts the range with an <code>AssertCount</code> over an overflow flag. An <code>int</code> sum is taken in <code>Int64</code>. A <code>long</code> total is checked from the sums of each value&#39;s 32-bit halves (<code>hi = x // 2^32</code>, <code>lo = x - hi * 2^32</code>, neither sum can overflow): it fits when <code>sum(hi) + sum(lo) // 2^32</code> fits an <code>int</code>, and the engine&#39;s wrapping sum is then exact. A distinct grouped <code>long</code> sum, whose halves cannot be deduplicated apart, is taken in <code>Decimal(38, 0)</code>. Either way the check is on the total, not on each partial sum, so <code>[int.MaxValue, 1, -1]</code> sums to <code>int.MaxValue</code> here, where LINQ to Objects throws. Without assertions, a grouped sum is narrowed with a strict cast instead (the engine&#39;s error). A decimal sum is not checked.</p><p><strong>Casts.</strong> A non-strict <code>Cast</code> between numeric types wraps on overflow, as .NET&#39;s unchecked conversion does: <code>(byte)300</code> is 44, where the engine would give null. A floating value converted to an integer truncates and saturates to the target&#39;s range for every integer type, as .NET 11 does: NaN is 0, and values beyond the range give MinValue / MaxValue, so <code>(byte)-2.7</code> is 0 and <code>(byte)300.5</code> is 255. On .NET 10 and earlier, LINQ to Objects reaches a type narrower than <code>int</code> through <code>int</code> and wraps (<code>(byte)-2.7</code> is 254); the provider deliberately follows .NET 11. Integer-to-integer narrowing still wraps. The frontend emits this saturation.</p><p><strong>Date and time.</strong> A <code>DateTimeOffset</code> is stored as a UTC instant, so its members read the UTC value: <code>Offset</code> is zero, and <code>Hour</code> and <code>DateTime</code> are the UTC hour and time.</p><h2 id="strings-and-membership" tabindex="-1">Strings and membership <a class="header-anchor" href="#strings-and-membership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strings and membership&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Null as the empty string.</strong> <code>StringConcat</code>, <code>ConcatString</code>, <code>FormatString</code> and <code>ListJoin</code> follow .NET <code>String.Concat</code>, <code>String.Format</code> and <code>String.Join</code>. A null operand, argument or element contributes <code>&quot;&quot;</code>. The result is never null on account of a null operand.</p><p><strong>Ordinal string order.</strong> Strings compare and sort ordinally, by code point (the UTF-8 byte order), not by culture: <code>&quot;Banana&quot; &lt; &quot;apple&quot; &lt; &quot;äpfel&quot;</code>. This holds for <code>Sort</code>, comparisons, <code>Min</code> / <code>Max</code> and the frontend&#39;s <code>CompareTo</code> (-1 / 0 / 1). .NET defaults to the current culture, so results can differ from LINQ to Objects. A query that passes a comparer (<code>StringComparer.CurrentCulture</code>, for instance) runs as LINQ to Objects under the client evaluation policy (see Untranslated operators). Code points above U+FFFF sort after U+E000-U+FFFF here, while .NET&#39;s ordinal comparison orders UTF-16 code units.</p><p><strong>The <code>nullsEqual</code> flag.</strong> <code>InList</code>, <code>ListContains</code> and <code>IsIn</code> carry <code>nullsEqual</code>.</p><ul><li>When true, a null matches a null element, as in .NET <code>Contains</code>.</li><li>When false, a null value gives null, as in SQL <code>IN</code>.</li></ul><p>The flag is always explicit. A backend never chooses it.</p><h2 id="exceptional-cases" tabindex="-1">Exceptional cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#exceptional-cases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exceptional cases&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Null propagation.</strong> Where .NET would throw, the IR propagates null, as EF Core does. Examples:</p><ul><li>a member or method called on a null string;</li><li>an index out of range (<code>ListGet</code>, <code>SeriesGet</code>);</li><li>a failed parse (<code>StrToDate</code>, <code>StrToInteger</code>).</li></ul><p>Strict materialization then reports a null that reaches a non-nullable member.</p><p><strong><code>AssertCount</code>.</strong> The nodes that raise are <code>ScalarFunction.AssertCount</code> (<code>[count; value]</code>) and <code>AggKind.CheckedSum</code> (see Checked sums). <code>AssertCount</code> yields the value when the count (any 64-bit integer: a row count, an overflow flag) lies in range. Otherwise the query fails with the check&#39;s <code>QueryError</code>, which the backend surfaces as the matching .NET exception. It has an effect, so it must be evaluated wherever its value is used, and never constant-folded. A backend that cannot raise answers <code>IBackendCapabilities.SupportsAssertions = false</code>. The frontend then evaluates such subqueries at compile time and marks the compilation so its plan is not cached.</p><p><strong>No short-circuit.</strong> Expressions are evaluated whole: both sides of <code>And</code> / <code>Or</code> and both branches of a <code>Conditional</code>, for every row. An <code>AssertCount</code> is therefore checked once whenever its plan runs, even where the logic around it (<code>false AND ...</code>, an empty outer relation) would never read its value. This is stricter than LINQ to Objects, never laxer: whenever LINQ raises, so does the query.</p><p><strong>Guarded UDFs.</strong> The frontend restores C# short-circuit evaluation for managed UDFs. A UDF on the right of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> / <code>||</code> / <code>??</code>, or in a <code>?:</code> branch, receives the guard (the left side, its negation, the left&#39;s null test, the test or its negation) as one more input. It is invoked only on rows where the guard is true, and yields null elsewhere, which the guarding operator discards. A guard that aggregates or reads a window is not applied.</p><p><strong>Untranslated operators.</strong> A query operator the frontend does not translate (an overload with a comparer, an unknown operator) sends the whole query to LINQ to Objects under the client evaluation policy. It is never skipped.</p><h2 id="rows-and-order" tabindex="-1">Rows and order <a class="header-anchor" href="#rows-and-order" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rows and order&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Order is kept unless a node says otherwise.</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>Filter</code>, <code>Project</code>, <code>WithColumns</code>, <code>Rename</code>, <code>WithRowIndex</code> and <code>Explode</code> keep input order.</p></li><li><p><code>Sort</code> is stable. <code>NullsLast</code> places nulls. With <code>AllowUnstableOrdering</code>, the order of tied rows is unspecified, so the order of a sort&#39;s input is not observed and the optimizer may reorder the joins and groupings beneath it.</p></li><li><p><code>Union</code> lists the left rows, then the right. Its inputs have the same column names, which a backend matches by name; only single-column inputs are matched by position. When an object element&#39;s sides assign different members, the frontend completes each side with the other members at their type&#39;s default.</p></li><li><p><code>Distinct</code> and <code>GroupBy</code> keep first-appearance order only with <code>maintainOrder</code>. <code>GroupOrderRelaxation</code> clears that flag where nothing observes the order.</p></li><li><p>Joins:</p><ul><li><code>Inner</code> and <code>Left</code> keep left order, then right order among a row&#39;s matches.</li><li><code>Semi</code> and <code>Anti</code> keep left order.</li><li><code>Cross</code> has no order.</li></ul><p>Constant-key left joins rely on this to attach one-row plans without reordering.</p></li><li><p><code>Limit</code> takes <code>count</code> rows after <code>offset</code>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Expressions that read other rows.</strong> Window, rank, cumulative and other frame functions (<code>ScalarExprOps.dependsOnOtherRows</code>) are evaluated over the whole input of the <code>Project</code> / <code>WithColumns</code> that computes them. A <code>Filter</code> above that node never changes their values, so predicate pushdown must not move a filter below it.</p><p><strong>Order-reading aggregates.</strong> <code>First</code>, <code>Last</code> and <code>List</code> read their input&#39;s order, so a pass must keep every order guarantee beneath them. <code>GroupOrderRelaxation</code> frees order only under order-free aggregates.</p><p><strong>Aggregates skip nulls.</strong></p><ul><li><code>Sum</code> over no values is 0.</li><li><code>Mean</code>, <code>Min</code>, <code>Max</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>Last</code> and <code>Median</code> over no values are null.</li><li><code>Count</code> counts non-null values. <code>Len</code> counts rows.</li><li><code>Std</code> / <code>Var</code> with ddof d are null with at most d values.</li><li>A <code>Project</code> whose projections all aggregate yields exactly one row, even over no input rows. A <code>GroupBy</code> over no rows yields no rows, and a <code>GroupingSets</code> level with an empty key subset still yields its one grand-total row.</li></ul><p><strong>GroupBy <code>having</code>.</strong> <code>having</code> is an aggregate predicate over a group&#39;s rows. It keeps the groups for which it is true, before anything above the GroupBy runs.</p><h2 id="reshaping" tabindex="-1">Reshaping <a class="header-anchor" href="#reshaping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reshaping&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong><code>Explode</code></strong> produces one row per list element, rows in input order and elements in list order. Several columns explode together and pair their elements, so each row&#39;s lists must have equal lengths, or the query fails. The frontend explodes one column at a time.</p><ul><li>An empty list gives one null row with <code>emptyAsNull</code>, otherwise no row.</li><li>A null list gives one null row with <code>keepNulls</code>, otherwise no row.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong><code>Unpivot</code></strong> outputs the columns <code>index..., variable, value</code>. It produces one block of rows per <code>on</code> column, in <code>on</code> order, and each block lists rows in input order.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>JoinAsOf</code></strong> keeps every left row once, in left order. Each left row gets the right row whose <code>on</code> key is nearest by the strategy, among the right rows with equal <code>by</code> keys:</p><ul><li>Backward: the greatest key &lt;= the left key;</li><li>Forward: the least key &gt;=;</li><li>Nearest: the closest key.</li></ul><p>Without <code>AllowEq</code>, <code>&lt;=</code> / <code>&gt;=</code> become <code>&lt;</code> / <code>&gt;</code>. When no row qualifies, the right columns are null. A null <code>on</code> or <code>by</code> key never matches, on either side. Both inputs are sorted by their <code>on</code> keys.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>GroupingSets</code></strong> produces one block per level, in <code>Levels</code> order.</p><ul><li>A level groups by its subset of keys. Groups appear in first-key order, and an empty subset gives one grand-total row.</li><li>Keys outside the subset are null.</li><li>Each level&#39;s own literals set their columns.</li></ul></li></ul><h2 id="joins-and-keys" tabindex="-1">Joins and keys <a class="header-anchor" href="#joins-and-keys" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Joins and keys&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A null join key matches a null key exactly when the join&#39;s <code>nullsEqual</code> is true.</p><ul><li>Explicit LINQ joins use false (<code>Enumerable.Join</code>). A composite key is one key column per component, so a null component never matches, as in EF Core (LINQ to Objects matches equal anonymous keys, null components included).</li><li>Decorrelated subqueries use true, or false under <code>UseRelationalNulls</code>.</li><li>A <code>GroupJoin</code> with a result selector is compiled as a projection whose group is a correlated subquery (<code>inner.Where(b =&gt; key(b) != null &amp;&amp; key(b) == key(a))</code>): with no null inner key component, a null key matches nothing, as in <code>Enumerable.GroupJoin</code>.</li><li>Outer joins (<code>LeftJoin</code>, <code>RightJoin</code>, <code>FullJoin</code>): an unmatched side is null when its type is a reference type, and <code>default(T)</code> when it is a non-nullable struct, as in LINQ to Objects. The frontend marks each such struct side with a literal <code>true</code> column before the join, and after it rewrites the side&#39;s non-nullable value members to <code>marker is null ? default : member</code>. Reference and <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code> members stay null. A matched row&#39;s own null is not replaced, so strict materialization still reports it.</li><li>.NET 11 tuple joins (no result selector, yielding <code>(Outer, Inner)</code>) are compiled with the implied selector <code>(o, i) =&gt; (o, i)</code>, so <code>pair.Outer</code> / <code>pair.Inner</code> (<code>Item1</code> / <code>Item2</code> in the tree) resolve to their sides.</li><li>A <code>Nullable&lt;struct&gt;</code> side (<code>Select(x =&gt; (T?)x)</code> before the join) is not folded to <code>default(T)</code>: unmatched, it is null. <code>.Value</code> is a transparent navigation member, and <code>.HasValue</code> is the side&#39;s presence, the same test as <code>side != null</code> for a class (a non-nullable value member of the side is not null).</li><li>The same probe serves the row itself in a single-entity lambda (<code>src.Select(x =&gt; (T?)x).Where(x =&gt; x.HasValue)</code>, <code>x != null</code> on a class row). Accepted deviation: a null in that member&#39;s column (data T cannot hold) reads as an absent row instead of failing strict materialization. A type with no non-nullable value member is not probed (the check runs on the client). Entities are flat columns without a presence bit, so the materializer reads an all-null row as a null <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code>; a present struct whose members are all null is indistinguishable.</li></ul><h2 id="aggregates-over-defaultifempty" tabindex="-1">Aggregates over DefaultIfEmpty <a class="header-anchor" href="#aggregates-over-defaultifempty" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Aggregates over DefaultIfEmpty&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In correlated subqueries (and so in <code>GroupJoin</code> groups), <code>Agg(s.DefaultIfEmpty(d))</code> is <code>s is empty ? Agg([d]) : Agg(s)</code>:</p><ul><li>For a non-nullable value element type T, <code>Max</code> / <code>Min</code> / <code>Average</code> become <code>s.Select(x =&gt; (T?)x).Agg() ?? (R)d</code>: an aggregate over a nullable sequence is null exactly when the sequence is empty. <code>Sum</code> with the default <code>d</code> is <code>Sum(s)</code>.</li><li>For a nullable or reference element type with no <code>d</code> (or a null <code>d</code>), the call is <code>Agg(s)</code>.</li><li>Other shapes (a selector on the aggregate, a non-default <code>d</code> for <code>Sum</code>, <code>Count</code>) are not rewritten.</li></ul>',61)])])}const g=o(s,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
